# Pipeline Tests
#
# These exercise the |, |&, and pipeline-plus-job-control features described
# in ish.man.pdf. The data-flow checks use exact matching since cat's output
# is fully predictable; the backgrounded-pipeline checks use shelltest's
# regexp mode (a bare `/pattern/` after `>`, which searches for the pattern
# anywhere in the actual output) since job numbers and process IDs can't be
# predicted. See job-control.test for why exact wording of job status
# messages isn't checked.
#
# A pipeline-plus-SIGTSTP test lives in pipeline-signals.sh instead, since
# shelltest can't deliver a real stop signal (see job-control.test).

# Test that a two-stage pipeline connects the first command's stdout to the
# second command's stdin
<
/bin/cat redirectin.txt | /bin/cat
$ SHELL
>
PROMPT Contents of the file redirectin
PROMPT exit

# Test a pipeline with many stages (the assignment README calls for support
# up to 10 processes)
<
/bin/echo relay | /bin/cat | /bin/cat | /bin/cat | /bin/cat | /bin/cat | /bin/cat | /bin/cat | /bin/cat | /bin/cat
$ SHELL
>
PROMPT relay
PROMPT exit

# Test that a plain | pipeline connects only the first command's stdout to
# the next command's stdin; its stderr still goes to the terminal
<
/bin/chmod 700 ./pipesplit.sh
./pipesplit.sh | /bin/cat
$ SHELL
>
PROMPT PROMPT stdout-line
PROMPT exit
>2
stderr-line

# Test that |& connects both the first command's stdout and stderr into the
# pipeline
<
./pipesplit.sh |& /bin/cat
$ SHELL
>
PROMPT stdout-line
stderr-line
PROMPT exit

# Test that backgrounding a pipeline reports the job number and the process
# ID of every stage, not just one
<
/bin/sleep 1 | /bin/cat &
$ SHELL
> /\[1\] [0-9]+ [0-9]+/

# Test that jobs lists a backgrounded pipeline as a single job
<
/bin/sleep 2 | /bin/cat &
jobs
$ SHELL
> /\[1\].*sleep/
